---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";

const layering = `schema defaults  <  defaults.yml  <  repo .ci-doctor.yml  <  CI_DOCTOR_* env  <  CLI flags`;

const envExample = `# Nested keys use a double underscore:
export CI_DOCTOR_LLM__MODEL="qwen2.5-coder:32b"
export CI_DOCTOR_LLM__API_BASE="http://vllm.internal:8000/v1"
export CI_DOCTOR_CI="github"

# Secret-looking single-underscore vars (e.g. CI_DOCTOR_GITLAB_TOKEN) are
# referenced by NAME (token_env) and ignored by the config loader.`;

const inspect = `ci-doctor config           # the effective config, every layer merged
ci-doctor config --diff    # only what your file/env/flags change vs the defaults
ci-doctor config --schema  # the JSON Schema, for editors and CI validation
ci-doctor config --validate            # merge every layer and report what fails
ci-doctor config -f base.yml -f ci.yml # repeatable, the rightmost file wins
# Output pages through $PAGER on a terminal; --plain prints raw (as does piping).`;

const schemaComment = `# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://github.com/fennet82/ci-doctor/releases/latest/download/ci-doctor.schema.json
llm:
  model: qwen2.5-coder:32b`;

const full = `# .ci-doctor.yml — every knob, with defaults shown.
ci: github                       # which CI to read the run from (github | gitlab)
scm: null                        # which git host gets the note (defaults to "ci")

gitlab:
  base_url: https://gitlab.com   # default; override for a self-hosted instance
  api_version: v4
  token_env: CI_DOCTOR_GITLAB_TOKEN
  token_file: null               # e.g. /run/secrets/gitlab_token (wins over token_env)
  ca_bundle: null                # path to a custom/internal CA bundle
  verify_ssl: true
  timeout_seconds: 30

github:                          # used when github is the ci or the scm
  base_url: https://api.github.com     # default; override with a GHE base, e.g. https://ghe/api/v3
  token_env: CI_DOCTOR_GITHUB_TOKEN
  token_file: null
  ca_bundle: null
  verify_ssl: true
  timeout_seconds: 30

llm:
  enabled: true                  # false => deterministic-only report
  backend: openai                # openai | anthropic | azure | bedrock | litellm
  model: null                    # e.g. "qwen2.5-coder:32b" or "claude-opus-4-8"
  api_base: null                 # OpenAI-compatible / litellm base URL
  api_key_env: null              # optional; local servers often need none
  ca_bundle: null                # LLM endpoint's own CA, independent of the provider's
  azure_endpoint: null           # required for backend: azure
  azure_api_version: null        # required for backend: azure
  aws_region: null               # backend: bedrock; falls back to AWS_DEFAULT_REGION/AWS_REGION
  max_input_tokens: 12000
  temperature: 0.1
  timeout_seconds: 120
  max_retries: 1                 # HTTP-transport retries; Pydantic AI's own Agent retries once more on an invalid reply

analysis:
  include_allowed_failures: false   # allow_failure jobs are noise by default
  max_jobs_analyzed: 10
  max_parallel_jobs: 4              # jobs analyzed at once; 1 for a strictly sequential run
  skip_llm_for: [no_runner, missing_dependency, cancelled]  # fully-determined -> templated

phases:                          # section/step name -> phase (merged onto the shipped map)
  my_custom_deploy_section: script

extraction:
  tail_lines: 120
  matchers:                      # anchored windows; higher priority survives budget pressure
                                 # Merged by id onto the shipped language packs: a new
                                 # id adds a pack, a shipped id retunes one (and warns).
    - id: my_service_errors      # new id -> added alongside the shipped packs
      pattern: '^(ERROR|FATAL)\\b'
      before: 5
      after: 20
      priority: 50
    - id: pytest                 # shipped id -> only these fields change, the rest is inherited
      priority: 90

denoise:
  collapse_carriage_returns: true   # collapse \\r progress bars to their final state
  dedupe_repeats: true              # repeated lines -> "<line>  (×47)"
  noise_patterns:
    - '^\\s*$'

output:
  terminal: true
  markdown_path: report.md
  json_path: report.json
  mr_note: false                 # post/update a note on the MR/PR (needs a live pipeline)

redaction:
  enabled: true
  extra_patterns: []             # additional regexes to scrub`;
---

<Base title="Configuration">
    <h1>Configuration</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        One layered, pydantic-validated config. Unknown keys are an error, not a
        silent typo.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
        This page is the reference — every key and its default. For what the
        keys
        <em>mean</em> — phases, attribution, denoising, matchers, budget, redaction
        — start with
        <a href={url("/concepts/")}>Concepts</a>. The shipped matcher packs are
        listed on
        <a href={url("/concepts/matchers/")}>Matchers</a>.
    </div>

    <h2>How layers combine</h2>
    <Code code={layering} lang="text" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        Mappings deep-merge; lists and scalars replace. The one exception is a
        list whose entries all carry an <code>id</code> — today only <code
            >extraction.matchers</code
        > — which merges per id, so your packs add to the shipped ones instead of
        wiping them. Point at specific files with <code
            >-f path/to/.ci-doctor.yml</code
        >, repeated as often as you like — each one overrides the last.
    </p>

    <h2>Inspecting the result</h2>
    <p>Config bugs are almost always a layer you forgot was applied. Ask:</p>
    <Code code={inspect} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        <code>--diff</code> reads like <code>git diff</code>: green is what your
        config adds, red is the default it replaced.
    </p>

    <h2>Editor completion</h2>
    <p>
        Every release ships <code>ci-doctor.schema.json</code>, generated from
        the same pydantic model that validates your file, so it can never drift.
        Point your editor at it:
    </p>
    <Code code={schemaComment} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />

    <h2>Environment variables</h2>
    <Code code={envExample} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />

    <h2>LLM backends</h2>
    <p>
        The LLM step is bring-your-own, via <a href="https://ai.pydantic.dev/">Pydantic
        AI</a>. Pick one with <code>llm.backend</code>:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead
            ><tr><th>backend</th><th>What it calls</th><th>Needs</th></tr
            ></thead
        >
        <tbody>
            <tr
                ><td><code>openai</code> (default)</td><td
                    >Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp,
                    internal gateway) or OpenAI itself</td
                ><td
                    ><code>model</code> + <code>api_base</code>; <code
                        >pip install ci-doctorr[openai]</code
                    ></td
                ></tr
            >
            <tr
                ><td><code>anthropic</code></td><td
                    >The Anthropic Messages API directly</td
                ><td
                    ><code>model</code> + <code>api_key_env</code>; <code
                        >pip install ci-doctorr[anthropic]</code
                    ></td
                ></tr
            >
            <tr
                ><td><code>azure</code></td><td
                    >Azure OpenAI</td
                ><td
                    ><code>model</code>, <code>azure_endpoint</code>, <code
                        >api_key_env</code
                    >; <code>pip install ci-doctorr[openai]</code></td
                ></tr
            >
            <tr
                ><td><code>bedrock</code></td><td
                    >Amazon Bedrock, AWS IAM auth (env vars, profile, or instance
                    role) — not an API key</td
                ><td
                    ><code>model</code>; <code>aws_region</code> or
                    <code>AWS_DEFAULT_REGION</code>; <code
                        >pip install ci-doctorr[bedrock]</code
                    ></td
                ></tr
            >
            <tr
                ><td><code>litellm</code></td><td
                    >Whatever else <a href="https://docs.litellm.ai/">litellm</a>
                    reaches — Vertex, Cohere, watsonx, custom proxies, ~100
                    providers total</td
                ><td
                    ><code>model</code> in litellm's own
                    <code>provider/model</code> form; <code
                        >pip install ci-doctorr[litellm]</code
                    ></td
                ></tr
            >
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="note">
        <strong>Reach for <code>openai</code> first.</strong> The OpenAI Chat
        Completions shape is what Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp, Together, Groq, DeepSeek,
        Mistral and most others already speak. There is no default SDK in the base
        install any more — every backend needs its own extra, picked for exactly the
        provider you're using.
    </div>
    <div class="note">
        <strong><code>openai</code>/<code>azure</code> and <code>litellm</code>
        are mutually exclusive installs, not a menu to combine.</strong> litellm
        hard-pins <code>openai&lt;3.0</code>, while Pydantic AI's own OpenAI
        integration needs <code>openai&gt;=3.8</code> — the two cannot be installed
        in one environment. Pick whichever track covers your provider;
        <code>anthropic</code> and <code>bedrock</code> are unaffected either way.
        <code>litellm</code> may
        also pull <code>tiktoken</code>, which fetches vocab at runtime, so it is
        unfit for a strict air gap. When the LLM is disabled or unavailable,
        ci-doctor emits the deterministic report instead of failing.
    </div>
    <div class="note">
        <strong><code>bedrock</code> with no resolvable AWS credentials can probe
        the network at startup.</strong> Unlike the other backends, building the
        client eagerly resolves AWS credentials (env vars, profile, or instance
        metadata) — with none available anywhere, that resolution itself makes a
        request rather than failing instantly. Set <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code>/<code
            >AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code
        > (or run with an instance role) so it never reaches that path.
    </div>

    <h2>Full reference</h2>
    <p>
        Drop a <code>.ci-doctor.yml</code> at your repo root. Everything below is
        optional — every key has a default.
    </p>
    <Code code={full} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />

    <div class="note">
        <strong><code>base_url</code> defaults to the public host</strong> (<code
            >gitlab.com</code
        > /
        <code>api.github.com</code>). Override it for a self-hosted GitLab or
        GitHub Enterprise instance. Custom CA bundles and proxies are honoured
        either way.
    </div>
</Base>
